116
00:07:23,889 --> 00:07:28,066
Welcome to the chapter two,
Versioned Interface Contract.

117
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:33,033
In a real super app, teams should,
should need to talk to each other.

118
00:07:33,113 --> 00:07:37,467
For example, when the consumers or
customers is setting up routers in

119
00:07:37,468 --> 00:07:43,800
the IoT features need to pay a fee
activation, or IoT model needs to open

120
00:07:43,802 --> 00:07:48,841
the payment screens, or when a loyalty
shows Tuesday's rewards, it need to ask

121
00:07:48,872 --> 00:07:50,682
account models for the user profile.

122
00:07:51,636 --> 00:07:54,894
In the old world, the loyalty
team could directly import

123
00:07:55,045 --> 00:07:56,830
account team's internal classes.

124
00:07:57,193 --> 00:08:00,308
The moment you do that, those
two teams are glued together.

125
00:08:00,941 --> 00:08:05,189
To break that glue, we introduce
versions interface contract.

126
00:08:06,101 --> 00:08:10,700
Let's look at the next
slide to see how that works.

127
00:08:11,514 --> 00:08:15,556
In this slide, you can see the three
simple rules that makes our contract work.

128
00:08:16,363 --> 00:08:17,236
Typed interface.

129
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:20,197
Splitting API from implementations.

130
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:25,572
Every team splits their module in two
parts: a tiny contract module, the public

131
00:08:25,577 --> 00:08:30,532
API, a private implementations where
actual screens and business logics live.

132
00:08:31,331 --> 00:08:35,205
The contract module only contains a
simple interface and a data module.

133
00:08:35,768 --> 00:08:38,737
No UI, no databases or no heavy logic.

134
00:08:39,274 --> 00:08:42,997
Because it is pure interfaces, it
compiles in less than a second.

135
00:08:43,951 --> 00:08:48,985
And here's our number one rule:
teams are only allowed to depend on

136
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:52,838
other teams' contract modules, never
on their implementation modules.

137
00:08:54,149 --> 00:08:56,649
The second part to this
one is semantic version.

138
00:08:57,832 --> 00:09:01,614
Second, we versions our every
contract using a semantic versions.

139
00:09:01,876 --> 00:09:06,325
If the account teams add a new helper
method that doesn't break anything,

140
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:10,759
they bump their minor versions,
nobody else has to touch their code.

141
00:09:11,484 --> 00:09:15,495
If they need to make a breaking
change, they cannot just delete the old

142
00:09:15,502 --> 00:09:18,110
method or break all other four teams.

143
00:09:18,794 --> 00:09:23,457
They mark the old method as deprecated,
release a new version of the contract, and

144
00:09:23,466 --> 00:09:26,270
give other teams time to migrate smoothly.

145
00:09:27,342 --> 00:09:30,233
The third part is like
consumers independence.

146
00:09:30,776 --> 00:09:35,714
Because other teams only depends on
your public interface, your team can

147
00:09:35,895 --> 00:09:40,890
completely rewrite your internal code,
upgrade your databases, or redesign

148
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:44,896
your UI screens without asking or
notifying any other team, as long

149
00:09:44,898 --> 00:09:46,253
as the contract stays the same.

150
00:09:46,973 --> 00:09:50,315
Let's move on to the next slide to
see how this speed up our builds.

151
00:09:51,315 --> 00:09:55,293
Look at the factory floor on the
slide and notice how this isn't

152
00:09:55,337 --> 00:09:57,488
