with fifty-thir-fifty-three million
active users, how do you keep everything

235
00:14:41,747 --> 00:14:43,564
safe, consistent, and crash free?

236
00:14:44,766 --> 00:14:47,093
There are two big risks
when team moves fast.

237
00:14:47,398 --> 00:14:51,877
First, under tight deadlines, developer
might accidentally bypass the rule

238
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:53,394
and import code they shouldn't.

239
00:14:54,121 --> 00:14:58,545
Second, even if the code imports are
clean, if two models mess with the shared

240
00:14:58,547 --> 00:15:03,505
memory and a glob-global data runtime,
the app can still freeze or crash.

241
00:15:04,101 --> 00:15:07,840
Let's look at the next slide,
how we can protect the platform

242
00:15:07,842 --> 00:15:09,442
without slowing teams down

243
00:15:10,442 --> 00:15:14,188
This slide shows the two ways we
can protect stability, automated

244
00:15:14,188 --> 00:15:16,440
governance and the reactive state.

245
00:15:17,574 --> 00:15:19,396
Let's see like in governance model.

246
00:15:19,987 --> 00:15:25,088
Nobody likes to slow architecture review
boards where you have to wait for two

247
00:15:25,089 --> 00:15:27,366
weeks or for the permission to merge code.

248
00:15:27,759 --> 00:15:28,715
Our rule is simple.

249
00:15:29,246 --> 00:15:33,173
The shell team owns the platform contract
and without blocking team velocity.

250
00:15:33,872 --> 00:15:38,772
Instead of slow meetings, we use
three lightweight tools: an RFC

251
00:15:38,772 --> 00:15:40,843
process for contract changes.

252
00:15:41,024 --> 00:15:45,422
If you change code inside your own
module, you need zero outside approvals.

253
00:15:46,142 --> 00:15:50,242
You only write a short RFC document if you
want to change a shared public contract.

254
00:15:50,996 --> 00:15:52,277
Automated lint rules.

255
00:15:53,110 --> 00:15:56,012
We don't rely on humans catching
mistake in code reviews.

256
00:15:56,062 --> 00:15:59,802
If your developer in loyalty
accidentally tried to import a private

257
00:15:59,809 --> 00:16:04,097
code from payment, our automated
linter catches it in two seconds and

258
00:16:04,097 --> 00:16:05,870
stop the pull request automatically.

259
00:16:06,868 --> 00:16:08,071
Shared ADR log.

260
00:16:08,690 --> 00:16:13,205
We keep a simple log for architectural
design records right in the repo, so

261
00:16:13,207 --> 00:16:15,748
every engineers knows why our rules exist.

262
00:16:16,822 --> 00:16:19,062
The second part of this
one is a reactive state.

263
00:16:19,533 --> 00:16:23,191
On the right side, if you see the, we
protect the app at runtime using a clean

264
00:16:23,192 --> 00:16:26,518
state patterns, MVI and MVVM patterns.

265
00:16:27,448 --> 00:16:30,091
So unidirectional state flow.

266
00:16:30,186 --> 00:16:35,768
Inside our every module, data flows in one
clear direction, makes bug easy to trace.

267
00:16:36,416 --> 00:16:39,081
Shared event bus, never direct calls.

268
00:16:39,593 --> 00:16:43,251
When a user logs out an account
module, account module doesn't reach to

269
00:16:43,251 --> 00:16:45,048
payment or loyalty to clear the data.

270
00:16:45,884 --> 00:16:50,969
Instead, account publish a simple user
logged out event to a shared event bus.

271
00:16:51,045 --> 00:16:53,553
Every module clean up its own data safely.

272
00:16:54,469 --> 00:16:56,485
State scope to module's life cycle.

273
00:16:56,752 --> 00:17:01,286
As soon as user closes the IoT screen,
all other memory used by the IoT

274
00:17:01,294 --> 00:17:02,788
module is immediately cleared out.

275
00:17:03,541 --> 00:17:06,658
This is how we clean memory
usage and maintain ninety-nine

276
00:17:06,661 --> 00:17:08,292
point nine crash-free sessions.

277
00:17:09,521 --> 00:17:11,501
This bring to the--
that to the next slide.

278
00:17:13,046 --> 00:17:14,586
So what do you take back to your team?

279
00:17:15,586 --> 00:17:20,742
If you're scaling a large mobile app
or building an internal platform for

280
00:17:20,742 --> 00:17:24,334
your developer, here are the five
takeaways in this slide that you can

281
00:17:24,335 --> 00:17:26,263
start using, using it right away.

282
00:17:27,284 --> 00:17:29,338
First one, define your shell contract.

283
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:32,237
Stop treating your core
app as a shared bucket.

284
00:17:32,646 --> 00:17:37,551
Then turn your app shell into an internal
platform with clear and versioned API.

285
00:17:38,253 --> 00:17:39,562
Isolate build pipeline.

286
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:41,735
Let every module build and test their own.

287
00:17:41,865 --> 00:17:45,513
One team's red builds should never
block another team's green deploy.

288
00:17:46,322 --> 00:17:48,397
Build a feature injection registry.

289
00:17:48,987 --> 00:17:52,956
View feature teams as self-service
plug-and-play socket, way

290
00:17:52,957 --> 00:17:55,606
to add new screens without
touching in core shell code.

291
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:58,062
Adopt reactive state boundaries.

292
00:17:58,099 --> 00:18:04,054
Use MVI or MVVM with lifecycle scope
state and event bus so modules never

293
00:18:04,088 --> 00:18:05,972
corrupt each other's memory at runtime.

294
00:18:06,555 --> 00:18:08,535
Govern through contracts, not control.

295
00:18:09,121 --> 00:18:14,333
Use automated lint rules, simple RFCs to
protect architecture instead of slowing

296
00:18:14,338 --> 00:18:19,811
teams down with manual review committees
Platform engineering is not just for

297
00:18:19,841 --> 00:18:22,018
cloud servers and back-end infrastructure.

298
00:18:22,298 --> 00:18:26,535
Whenever you build clean boundaries,
self-serve tools, and automated

299
00:18:26,554 --> 00:18:31,436
guardrails, that helps engineering
ships scalably and safely.

300
00:18:32,029 --> 00:18:33,904
That is the mobile platform engineering.

301
